From 359f222201b344a1216ae4415808e4220ba4ecd5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: albanread <albanread@googlemail.com>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 09:59:40 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] MidiPlayer: an output selector, its own song player, a
 channel view

MidiPlayer could only play through the built-in synthesizer and crashed
when its window closed with a song loaded. It now has:

- Output: the built-in synthesizer or any MIDI consumer -- the Mac through
  the Prose MIDI device ("Mac (Prose MIDI)"), MIDI devices, other
  applications. Live input plays through the chosen output too.
- Its own song player (Song, SongPlayer): a Standard MIDI File parser
  (formats 0-2, RIFF RMID, running status, tempo map, SMPTE time) and a
  thread that sends each message through a Midi Kit 2 producer at its
  time. Pause, seek, stop and quitting wake it at once; seeking and
  switching outputs set the channels up as the song had them there
  (programs, controllers, pitch bend, system exclusive), and volume
  scales each channel's controller 7, so it works on any output.
- A channel view: the 16 channels with their General MIDI instruments and
  a bar that lights up with the notes -- for every output, where the scope
  only sees the built-in synthesizer (it stays, for that one).
- A position slider that seeks, elapsed and total time, Play/Pause, Stop,
  Loop, Tempo (25-200%), the song's title, a File menu with Open
  (starting in data/music), and "MidiPlayer --output <name> <file>".
- Settings keep the output, tempo and loop; the volume setting is read
  back into the volume at last (it was read into the window position).

The crash: BMidiSynthFile's player thread outlived it. BMidi::Stop() only
forgets the thread's id, and ~BMidi then waits for thread -1, which
returns at once; the thread wakes into the freed BMidiStore and calls the
song hook with the destroyed window, and the writes into freed memory
surfaced as a double free in ~BLooper. MidiPlayer no longer uses
BMidiSynthFile.

